NANOCRAFT — INSTALLATION INSTRUCTIONS
=======================================

REQUIREMENTS
-------------
- Python 3.10 or higher
- No specific operating system requirements (Linux, macOS, Windows all supported)
- pip (Python package installer)

The installation is designed to be minimal and straightforward.


1. DOWNLOAD THE SOURCE CODE
-----------------------------
Clone the repository with git:

    git clone https://github.com/jgrll/Nanocraft.git
    cd Nanocraft

Alternatively, download the ZIP archive directly from the GitHub repository page
and extract it:

    https://github.com/jgrll/Nanocraft


2. (RECOMMENDED) CREATE A VIRTUAL ENVIRONMENT
------------------------------------------------
While not strictly mandatory, it is recommended to create a dedicated Python
virtual environment to keep dependencies isolated from the rest of your system.

Using venv:

    python3 -m venv env
    source env/bin/activate        # Linux / macOS
    env\Scripts\activate           # Windows

Using conda:

    conda create -n env python=3.10
    conda activate env


3. INSTALL DEPENDENCIES
--------------------------
All required dependencies are listed in requirements.txt and can be installed
in a single command:

    pip install -r requirements.txt

If you want to use the toolkit on a remote machine, proceed to installation locally 
and then export the full folder (with the env).

4. LAUNCH NANOCRAFT
----------------------
Start Jupyter Notebook from the repository root:

    jupyter notebook

Then open the main notebook provided in the repository to start generating
and functionalizing nanoparticle structures. No further configuration is
required.

A separate visualization notebook is also included to inspect generated
structures, detected facets, and surface tessellation directly in your browser.

Make sure you choose the right virtual environment.


5. VERIFYING THE INSTALLATION
--------------------------------
To verify that Nanocraft and its dependencies were installed correctly, run
the first cells of the main notebook, which import the toolkit's modules.
If no import errors are raised, the installation is complete.


TROUBLESHOOTING
------------------
- If you encounter permission errors during pip install, make sure your
  virtual environment is activated (step 2) before running pip.
- If Jupyter is not recognized as a command, ensure it was installed as part
  of requirements.txt, or install it manually with:

    pip install notebook

For further help, please refer to the User Guide included in this repository,
or open an issue on the GitHub repository:
https://github.com/jgrll/Nanocraft/issues
